    "OtherAbstract": {
      "Type": "PIP",
      "Language": "eng",
      "AbstractText": "The number of female deaths in 1950-1957 due to cardiovascular disease is compared with the number from 1962 to 1969 to determine if use of oral contraceptives causes increased risk of thromboembolic disease. 30 women died in 1950-1957 and 51 died in 1962-1969, which showed a statistically significant increase. Of the 10 women who died from pulmonary embolism in 1962-1969, 4 had used oral contraceptives, and of the 19 women who died from coronary thrombosis in the same period, 2 had used oral contraceptives. 2 women who were using oral contraceptives died soon after the implantation of an IUD. The data are not large enough to indicate a causal relationship between the use of oral contraceptives and death from cardiovascular disease."
    }
  },
